

"Twenty-three dollars," he's saying. "Twenty-three dollars for the best dream of your life."

You don't know his name or anything -- he's just the guy you always meet at parties thrown by people you've never met. He's nobody's friend, exactly, but everyone knows who he is when it's time. Probably doesn't even have a name, probably has bagged groceries at some godforsaken Shopper's Food Warehouse since high school, but who cares? He's here to help you in your hour of need.

"What do you say?" he asks, holding up a repurposed cough syrup bottle with something green inside. Green like candy almost never is.

Stuck in an Attic
Sure, you're used to piece-of-shit architecture (you live in Dundalk, for crissake), but this place will be receiving some kind of award from you by the end of the night. There's totally unimportant crap strewn all over the place -- old sewing machines, an Easy-Bake oven, even a set of folding chairs painted carnival blue -- but fortunately, almost all of it is in shadow thanks to an underperforming fifty-watt bulb directly above.

It's just you and the dude here. The only place in the entire house you could get any privacy. It's only ten o'clock and already the call to hook up has come down from the heavens.

Stairs lead downward, back to the party and that awful trance-bop they keep playing.

>pay guy
The transaction happens quickly: no hesitations, no reconsiderations, no refunds.

"Drink it slowly," the guy advises but you're already shotgunning it, staring directly at the poor light bulb, so dim it won't even leave behind an afterimage on your retinas, so far away now that the sound of the party below takes five minutes to reach you, so far now that the world is turning blue as it recedes into the distance, so dim now that the wires in the bulb... what are they called?

Luminants? No, that isn't the right word, that's not even a word at all --
[...]

"Hey man, is your name Dante?" a voice is asking.

You sit up with a start. "Yeah."

The attic is different now but you couldn't say why. How. You don't know how it's different. You're sitting on the floor -- lying on it, more accurately -- and for a split second, everything's slowed down and peaceful and makes sense, even the Easy-Bake oven.

Then this guy with a dirty baseball cap (what the fuck? how cliched are we going to get tonight?) who's been standing over you has to start talking to you again and it fucks everything up. No sign of your esteemed drug merchant, of course. He's probably onto bigger and better prey.

"There's this girl who's been calling everyone's cell phones asking for you, man."

You frown.

"Here, talk to her, man, she's been freaking out on me the last fifteen minutes."

He presses his cell phone up to your ear, which is a nice thing to do considering your arms are completely numb for reasons you don't want to think about right now.

"Dante?" asks a voice you recognize in an instant, never mind the cell phone, never mind the years: it's Beatrice. If this were a movie, there'd be a convenient fade to black to allow you to reminisce about your shared past history, perhaps beginning with that sunrise six years ago -- but this isn't a movie, kiddo, and she says again:

"Dante, is that you?"

She's crying.

>>uu
You try to speak but the words come out all nonsense. Isn't that the final insult? Finally a cell phone conversation worth having and you can't even form a single damn word.

"Dante, I'm in trouble," she says.

>>uu
You try hard to enuniciate, to make sense, to at least convey some mixture of longing and surprise in your tone of voice, but you can't do it right.

"I... I can't explain it to you on the phone. But I need you to come help me. Do you remember where my house is?"

>>uu
Of course you remember where her fucking house is. You still remember every single thing about her. French toast for breakfast with enough syrup to drown yourself...

"It's important," Beatrice says. "Come as soon as you can."

There's a faint click, like a bird's beak tapping on a chalkboard, and then she isn't there anymore.

Your arms are working now -- you're pushing yourself up off the floor without even realizing it -- and so is your voice. "I've got to go see her," you're saying.

The guy with the cell phone gives you as serious a look as a frat boy can. "Dude, I've never met you before or anything, but can I give you some advice? If I were you, I wouldn't drive anywhere tonight? Not with the kind of pupils you've got."

He backs away from you, maybe waiting for a rebuttal. But there's no point in arguing with a frat boy who's managed to cobble together some kind of personal belief.

"Can you give me a ride?" you ask.

"Nah, man," he says, suddenly displaying an interest in the stairway. "I gotta get back downstairs. The dance won't dance itself, you know what I'm saying?"

If you were not at all on drugs right now, you'd be able to come up with a really killer line, but it doesn't matter. There's got to be somebody at this hoedown that can get you to Beatrice.

Not Exactly the Same Attic
It's the light that makes it different: somehow it's become even dimmer than before, so that everything is shadow but your own body and the stairs leading down. Who knows what's in the recesses of the dark? Probably plenty of useful things: things to keep your shoelaces tied, things to remind you of your dentist appointments -- even things that could save your own life. But who knows? It's just a pile of stuff you don't know how to sort through.

The music downstairs is insistent, even burning.

>down

Second Floor Landing
Christmas lights twist around the railing leading down. There are enough of them that the the walls of the stairway are the hue of fruit salad, the kind with tiny oranges, not the other kind, the kind with apple slices. Ugh. That awful shade of brown they turn once they're exposed to air for too long.

The music's louder now, and there are echoes of synthesizer burble and cymbal crashes. It's starting to make sense in your head.

Stairs go up and down, and there's a hallway to the west.

>down

In A Foyer, Among the Stoners
The music is incredibly loud, booming even, but that hasn't stopped some fucking stoners, too unmotivated to even get up and dance, from watching Dr. Who on the old TV set sitting against one wall. They're all slumped in different positions on an orange couch, staring idly at the newest adventures of a British dude in space.

To the west is what used to be the living room but is now a solid mass of people dancing, grooving, groping. They've set up one of those budget disco-ball projectors and because of it, you can't distinguish people at all in there. It's all just once big dance. The weird part is that it looks like there's a pole in the middle of the whole thing. What's the story on that?

The front door's to the south, and the kitchen -- which for some reason appears to be empty -- is north.

>north

Kitchen
An island of calm and fluorescence. The music is just muffled enough by the walls that you can't hear any of the words -- instead it's a steady humdrum. The kitchen's the usual first-apartment affair: aging white tile floor, 60's era cabinets. A dishwasher, maybe still functional beyond its design life, and a refrigerator humming softly. There's not much decor beyond the series of photographs of drunk people, love letters, and past-due notices stuck on the fridge.

To the south is the foyer.

There's a crash somewhere else in the house -- you can't tell where, exactly -- followed by a happy shout.

>open cabinet
A quick search of the cabinets reveals an unexciting collection of food: sugar, flour, salt... three boxes of fettucini... oh hey, look, there's a flashlight in here. Why do people keep emergency supplies in their kitchen, anyway? You do it too, but thinking over it now, it doesn't really make sense.

>take flashlight
Taken.

>turn it on
You click the flashlight on.

>south

